Very IMPORTANT: 
When we serial print in mcu, then it keeps sending the data, while the remaining processes keep happening in the mcu.
When you call Serial.print("Hello"), your Arduino doesn't instantly push those characters out of the physical TX pin. Instead, it drops those bytes into an outgoing transmission buffer (a tiny waiting room in memory) and lets the background hardware slide them out bit-by-bit. This allows your main loop() code to keep running instantly without waiting for the slow serial hardware.

When you call Serial.flush(), it pauses (blocks) your entire program execution right at that line and refuses to move forward until that outgoing buffer is completely 
empty—meaning all data has physically left the TX pin.
So, use of Serial.flush() is to block the entire program till every bit in serial buffer has been sent away. After they have been sent, the program continues.

