Photoresistor or LDR(light detecting resistor):
Like thermistor, connect this to fixed resistor and make it as voltage divider.
No, it is exactly the opposite! In a standard photoresistor (LDR), when light increases, the internal resistance decreases drastically. So, higher voltage read or high 
resolution means, the resistance is high , so light is less.

Photodiode : It is connected in reverse bias, and when the p side of the diode is smaller in length and when light hits the p side, electrons become free and move towards the battery 
connected to n side. The current generated is small.So resistance high resistance is connected, for Arduino to measure the voltage. Photodiode module comes with resistor also.
These are very quick, unlike photoresistor, so they are used in super fast optical encoders.




















